      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "With no peroxide the addition is ionic and follows Markovnikov's rule. H+ adds to C1 to give the more stable secondary carbocation, and Br- then attacks C2, giving CH3CHBrCH3."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t06_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Only HBr shows the peroxide (Kharasch) effect. With HCl the H–Cl bond is too strong for homolysis. With HI the iodine radicals combine to form I2 instead of adding to the alkene."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t06_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Br• adds to C2 so that the radical forms on the benzylic carbon, which is resonance stabilised. H is then abstracted onto that carbon, giving C6H5-CH2-CHBr-CH3."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t06_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is false: HCl shows no peroxide effect, so the major product is 2-chloropropane. R is true: Br• adds to the terminal carbon, giving the secondary radical."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t06_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "blind_working": "I is correct: the Markovnikov route goes through a secondary carbocation and the anti-Markovnikov route through a secondary radical, both being the more stable intermediate. II is correct as the textbook (NCERT) explanation: iodine radicals combine to form I2 rather than add to the double bond."
    },
    {
