    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t08_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Only a terminal alkyne has an acidic sp C–H. Propyne (CH3C≡CH) reacts: CH3C≡CH + NaNH2 → CH3C≡CNa + NH3. Ethene, ethane and but-2-yne have no acidic hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t08_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Markovnikov hydration of propyne gives an enol, CH3C(OH)=CH2. This tautomerises to the ketone CH3COCH3, propanone."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t08_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Birch-type reduction with Na in liquid NH3 is an anti addition of hydrogen. It converts an internal alkyne to the trans-alkene, giving trans-but-2-ene."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t08_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Alkynes are less reactive than alkenes towards electrophilic addition, so A is false. The vinylic cation is less stable than an alkyl carbocation, so R is true."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t08_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "But-2-yne is an internal alkyne with no acidic hydrogen. It does not react with Na or with ammoniacal AgNO3, so I is false. Propyne adds 2 HBr by Markovnikov's rule to give CH3CBr2CH3, a gem-dihalide, so II is true."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t09_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Resonance makes every C-C bond in benzene equal at 139 pm. This lies between a C-C single bond (154 pm) and a C=C double bond (134 pm)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t09_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The cyclopentadienyl anion is cyclic, planar and fully conjugated with 6 pi electrons, so it obeys 4n+2 with n=1. Cyclooctatetraene has 8 pi electrons and is non-planar. Cyclopentadiene contains an sp3 carbon. Hexatriene is acyclic."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t09_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Naphthalene has 5 C=C bonds, giving 10 pi electrons. Setting 4n+2 = 10 gives n = 2."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t09_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Benzene is III (neutral aromatic, all bonds 139 pm). COT is I (8 pi electrons, tub-shaped). Tropylium is IV (aromatic cation, 6 pi electrons). Cyclopentadiene is II (has an sp3 carbon)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t09_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true. B is false because benzene gives only one 1,2-disubstituted isomer. C is true. D is false because COT has 8 pi electrons (4n) and is non-planar, so it is not aromatic. E is true."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t10_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "In mixed acid, H2SO4 protonates HNO3, which then loses water to give the nitronium ion NO2+. This is the electrophile that attacks benzene."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t10_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Electrophilic attack destroys the ring's aromaticity to form the arenium (sigma) complex. This step has the highest energy barrier and is rate-determining. The later deprotonation is fast."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t10_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Anhydrous FeCl3 acts as a Lewis acid that polarises Cl2 to generate the electrophile, giving ring substitution in the dark. UV light instead gives addition (BHC), and aqueous FeCl3 is deactivated."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t10_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The arenium ion is non-aromatic because conjugation is broken, so A is true. R is false: the carbon bearing both H and NO2 is sp3 hybridised, not sp2."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u15_t10_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "HNO3 + 2H2SO4 → NO2+ + H3O+ + 2HSO4- is the correct equation, so I is true. With excess nitrating mixture at higher temperature, the NO2 group directs the second substitution meta, giving m-dinitrobenzene, so II is true."
    },
